# Clean Code

## Naming

- Functions/methods: verbs or verb phrases (`collectTrafficData`, `ensureLogin`)
- Variables/types: nouns or noun phrases (`networkSnapshot`, `AlertRule`)
- No 1–2 character names except conventional loop indices in tiny scopes
- Prefer full words over cryptic abbreviations

## Functions and structure

- Keep functions small; extract when a block needs a comment to explain “why”
- One level of abstraction per function — do not mix SQL, HTTP response shaping, and CPE protocol in one long handler forever
- Prefer early returns over deep nesting
- DRY: extract shared helpers instead of copy-pasting session checks or DB init across routes

## Errors and control flow

```typescript
// BAD
try {
  await work();
} catch {}

// BAD — auth/hard failure must not look successful
return NextResponse.json({ data: defaults }); // when session is missing

// GOOD
try {
  await work();
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Failed to collect traffic', error);
  return NextResponse.json({ error: '采集失败' }, { status: 500 });
}
```

- Never silent `catch {}`
- Never return HTTP 200 for missing auth or unrecoverable failures
- Soft fallbacks (e.g. CPE down → historical DB) are OK only when intentional and documented in the response (`source`, `cpeError`)

## Types and comments

- New code: no new `as any` or `useState<any>` without a real interface
- Map DB rows / API bodies to typed interfaces at the boundary
- Comments only for non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or constraints — no narration

## Size guardrails

- Prefer splitting files that grow past ~300 lines of mixed concerns (page + chart + form + fetch)
- Prefer extending existing modules over parallel “utils” dumps
